Give `drivers/char/random.c` one narrow entropy input, so that rlxfw can own
the credit policy.

R7, 2026-09-30.  This is the ONLY vendor change the entropy fix needs.

THE DEFECT, 量.  Image `r6b8i` (recipe `3685a3a4`), over the console on
2026-09-30 (`bench/2026-09-30/SB-ENT.log`, `bench/2026-09-30/SB-RT1.log`):

    # cat /proc/sys/kernel/random/entropy_avail /proc/sys/kernel/random/poolsize
    0
    4096
    # cat /proc/uptime
    768.26                       (and 1613.27 on the second reading)

The pool is empty at 768 s and still empty at 1613 s.  The consequence is
R7's, not the kernel's: the broker's LOGIN and PWSET are specified fail-closed
(`NOENTROPY` until `entropy_avail >= 128` has been seen once), so as the
kernel stands this board can never issue a session token or salt a password.

THE MECHANISM, 讀, in the tree that builds
(`src-vendor/rtl819x-toolchain/linux-2.6.30`, `2.6.30.9`):

  * `drivers/char/random.c:524` `credit_entropy_bits()` is the only function
    that *raises* `input_pool.entropy_count` -- `account()` (`:790`, `:792`)
    debits it and `init_std_data()` (`:937`) zeroes it.  Its callers are `:666`
    inside `add_timer_randomness()` (`:614`), `:753` inside
    `xfer_secondary_pool()` -- which cannot credit `input_pool`, because
    `input_pool` has no `.pull` (`:422-428`) -- and the two root-only ioctls
    `RNDADDTOENTCNT` (`:1127`) and `RNDADDENTROPY` (`:1142`).
  * Three functions reach `add_timer_randomness()`: `add_input_randomness()`
    (`:673`), `add_interrupt_randomness()` (`:689`) and
    `add_disk_randomness()` (`:703`, behind `CONFIG_BLOCK`).
  * `add_interrupt_randomness()` is called from `kernel/irq/handle.c:429`,
    gated at `:428` on `IRQF_SAMPLE_RANDOM`, and gated again in the callee on
    `desc->timer_rand_state`, which only `rand_initialize_irq()` allocates --
    from `kernel/irq/manage.c:542`, inside the same flag test in
    `__setup_irq()`.  So setting the bit on an already-registered `irqaction`
    would not create the state either.  量: 36 driver and arch call sites in
    the drop set that flag and **zero of them is compiled into this image**;
    the positive control on that sweep is that the same regex over the same
    paths finds `IRQF_DISABLED` on 457 lines and `request_irq(` on 1,428.
  * 🔴 THE OTHER TWO PATHS ARE IDLE, NOT ABSENT.  An earlier draft of this
    header said "no keyboard, no mouse and no rotating disk"; both halves were
    wrong.  `drivers/input/keyboard/rtl819x-keys.c` registers a polled input
    device and calls `input_report_key()`, which reaches
    `add_input_randomness()` through `drivers/input/input.c:308` -- a **reset
    button press credits the pool**.  `add_disk_randomness()` is gated on
    `disk->random`, set unconditionally by `rand_initialize_disk()` at
    `block/genhd.c:1157` with no rotational test, so an mtdblock request
    completion would credit it (`drivers/mtd/mtd_blkdevs.c:122` ->
    `end_request` -> `block/blk-core.c:2011`); the image is quiet only because
    nothing issues mtdblock I/O, and `/dev/mtdN` through mtdchar does not use
    the block layer.  **Nothing credits the pool on an unattended boot** is the
    accurate claim, and it is the one the measurement above supports.

🔴 AND SETTING THE FLAG WOULD NOT HAVE BEEN A FIX.  `add_timer_randomness()`
mixes `get_cycles()` as the high-resolution half of its sample and derives its
credit from `jiffies` deltas alone -- and `arch/rlx/include/asm/timex.h`
defines `get_cycles()` as `return 0;` for this Lexra core.  On this board the
pool would receive a constant where the jitter belongs, and the credit would
be `min_t(int, fls(delta>>1), 11)` over the minimum of three orders of
difference of a 100 Hz counter, which is 0 for almost every pair of packets
arriving faster than a jiffy.  The flag would have produced a number.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES, AND WHY IT IS THIS SHAPE.  `mix_pool_bytes()` and
`credit_entropy_bits()` are `static` in this file and there is no other way
in; `add_input_randomness()` is exported but drags the jiffies estimator and a
`last_value` de-duplicator with it.  So this adds one function that mixes a
buffer and credits a number, and makes **no estimate of its own**.  The
estimate is the caller's, in `drivers/char/rlxfw-entropy.c`, where the
derating is written down and reviewable -- because over-crediting is a
security defect and it must not be possible to introduce one by changing a
vendor file.

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO.  It does not change any existing code path: no line is
modified or removed, `add_interrupt_randomness()` and the three `add_*`
functions behave exactly as before, and with no caller linked in the only
effect on the image is ~40 bytes of `.text`.  It does not include
`<linux/rlxfw-entropy.h>`, deliberately: a build without `--marks` stages no
rlxfw sources, and this patch must still apply and compile there.  It does not
make the bits cryptographically strong, and no value of `entropy_avail` can --
see `notes/entropy.md`.

⚠️ IT DOES MOVE EVERY LINE NUMBER IN THIS FILE.  The hunk adds 43 lines after
`:700`, so every `random.c` citation at or past `:701` shifts by +43 once this
patch is applied.  The citations in this header, in
`drivers/char/rlxfw-entropy.c` and in `notes/entropy.md` are all against the
PRISTINE drop, which is what `tools/citeresolve.py` and every other reading of
the vendor tree use.

REFUTED BY: a build in which `random.o` fails to compile; a `quiet` image in
which `entropy_avail` still reads 0 after the card of `notes/entropy.md` § 5
has driven traffic at the board; or any change in `/proc/interrupts` or in the
three `add_*` functions' behaviour attributable to this hunk.

